  "comments": [
    {
      "position": 0,
      "source_id": null,
      "text": "[kudasai] is an auxiliary verb which indicates a polite request: \"please do\"\n\n1) kudasai is a polite imperative form of [kudasaru], the honorofic version of [kureru] 'give'(me) and is used as an auxiliary verb with the te-form of verbs.\n　ゆっくり飲んでください。\n　please drink slowly/at your own pace\n\n2) The negtative question form [kudasaimasenka] makes a request more polite　\n　あした８時に来てくださいませんか。\n　Won't you come tomorrow at 8 O'Clock?\n\n3) [douzo] emphasizes the speakers request and makes it more polite.\n　どうぞたくさん食べてください。\n　Go ahead please, and eat as much as you like\n\n4) In very informal speech, kudasai may drop(this form of request is often used by female speakers)\n　はやく言って。\n　go on say it (please)\n\n　まだ帰らないで。\n　dont come back yet (please)\n\n5) [kure], the imperative form of kureru, can also be used in place of kudasai in informal male speech.[Vte/Vneg nai de] kure is the least polite request form.\n　いっしょに来てくれ。\n　can you come together (for me?)\n (=can you let me go with you?)\n\n　うちへ帰らないでくれ。\n don't come back to our place (OK?)",
      "html": "[kudasai] is an auxiliary verb which indicates a polite request: \"please do\"<br/><br/>1) kudasai is a polite imperative form of [kudasaru], the honorofic version of [kureru] 'give'(me) and is used as an auxiliary verb with the te-form of verbs.<br/>　ゆっくり飲んでください。<br/>　please drink slowly/at your own pace<br/><br/>2) The negtative question form [kudasaimasenka] makes a request more polite　<br/>　あした８時に来てくださいませんか。<br/>　Won't you come tomorrow at 8 O'Clock?<br/><br/>3) [douzo] emphasizes the speakers request and makes it more polite.<br/>　どうぞたくさん食べてください。<br/>　Go ahead please, and eat as much as you like<br/><br/>4) In very informal speech, kudasai may drop(this form of request is often used by female speakers)<br/>　はやく言って。<br/>　go on say it (please)<br/><br/>　まだ帰らないで。<br/>　dont come back yet (please)<br/><br/>5) [kure], the imperative form of kureru, can also be used in place of kudasai in informal male speech.[Vte/Vneg nai de] kure is the least polite request form.<br/>　いっしょに来てくれ。<br/>　can you come together (for me?)<br/> (=can you let me go with you?)<br/><br/>　うちへ帰らないでくれ。<br/> don't come back to our place (OK?)",
      "credits_raw": "ramu",
      "content_sha256": "d5eb5983627cf4ea730937a96a682e5501dd17e059d25c6d6de3bbbb50adc7d8",
      "original_created_at": null,
      "original_updated_at": null
    },
    {
      "position": 1,
      "source_id": null,
      "text": "Note : The object marker を is said 'o' but may be written as either 'wo' or 'o' depending on romaji system used.",
      "html": "Note : The object marker を is said 'o' but may be written as either 'wo' or 'o' depending on romaji system used.",
      "credits_raw": "Amatuka",
      "content_sha256": "5dd52c277daaab2273c57e9f0fb52dd7fcb4157b6665bbff4b300270c45da4fe",
      "original_created_at": null,
      "original_updated_at": null
    },
    {
      "position": 2,
      "source_id": null,
      "text": "ください can be used in two ways.\n1. To ask for an object をください ( wo kudasai )\n2. To ask for an action verb-te ください (v-te kudasai).",
      "html": "ください can be used in two ways.<br/>1. To ask for an object をください ( wo kudasai )<br/>2. To ask for an action verb-te ください (v-te kudasai).",
      "credits_raw": "Amatuka",
      "content_sha256": "7d154a63b29cf54b9ea768e3aa06771928b5c4026474daa34b4a85dff619f088",
      "original_created_at": null,
      "original_updated_at": null
    },
    {
      "position": 3,
      "source_id": null,
      "text": "Note みせる (miseru) is the dictionary form of the verb meaning 'to show'.\nみせて (misete) is the te form of the verb meaning 'to show'.",
      "html": "Note みせる (miseru) is the dictionary form of the verb meaning 'to show'.<br/>みせて (misete) is the te form of the verb meaning 'to show'.",
      "credits_raw": "Amatuka",
      "content_sha256": "14cb9ebd44fa97b7c91c0b0a4a323619087c75dcf7f7fa1da927074a35eb5328",
      "original_created_at": null,
      "original_updated_at": null
    },
